y interacting with the environment. In practice, it generally involves modeling curiosity as a cognitive drive, so the robot explores the environment to discover and learn novel goals. However, current approaches to curiosity exhibit several limitations. Curiosity is modeled as a top-down mechanism that is responsible for the management of goals and tracking their learning progress. This centralized view contrasts with recent theories of cognition and limits the understanding of curiosity on a neural level. In general, this problem induces a lack of ﬂexibility during the discovery and learning of goals. To address these issues, this thesis proposes investigating the early formation and representation of goals by modeling sensorimotor contingencies (SMCs) with a physical robotic arm. The theory of SMCs is a bottom-up approach to cognition for the early formation of sensorimotor b

